                    coach_default:          `Design decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_application:   `<strong>That's the right diagnosis.</strong> A program rated 4.6 didn't fail on ideas — it failed on application. No spaced practice, nothing rehearsed on the job, and outcomes never mapped to activities. Now you can fix what's actually broken instead of adding a fourth masterclass.`,
                    coach_r1_content:       `<strong>Your fix for low transfer is more or 'better' content.</strong> But the cohort already rated the content 4.6 — they didn't lack ideas, they lacked practice and application. You've made an intellectually inert program more intellectual. Where does a participant actually do the behaviour you want to change?`,
                    coach_r1_individuals:   `<strong>Careful — blaming the participants is the comfortable answer.</strong> When a whole cohort of able senior managers loves the program and changes nothing, that is a design problem, not a willpower problem. Diagnose the journey, not the people.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named it as an application gap AND aimed at the behavioural outcomes that lecture can't move — courageous decisions, coaching, cross-boundary influence. That is the lens that lets you redesign for what was actually missing.`,
                    coach_r1_cognitive:     `<strong>More frameworks won't move behaviour.</strong> The cognitive outcomes are the ones LTC already over-serves. The under-served outcomes — the behavioural ones — need rehearsal and feedback, not another model for ambiguity.`,
                    coach_r1_equal:         `<strong>Refusing to prioritise is how everything stays at 62%.</strong> If every outcome is equally important, none gets the practice time it needs. Name which outcomes are most under-served and aim the redesign at them.`,
                    coach_r1_lowcandor:     `<strong>This is how the redesign quietly becomes 'more content'.</strong> Low willingness to name a transfer problem means you'll add a lecture and call it a fix. Name the application gap honestly or you'll repeat the 41%.`,
                    coach_r2_balanced:      `<strong>That ratio holds the tension.</strong> Enough experience to drive transfer, enough framing to keep it coherent and engaging. This is the balance the journey was missing — not 70% theory, not a wall of unframed activity.`,
                    coach_r2_overstretch:   `<strong>You've maximised experiential method and lost engagement.</strong> At over 80% experiential the journey reads as relentless stretch — last year's pilot scored 3.2 and people stopped attending. Transfer doesn't happen if leaders disengage. Keep energy in the room.`,
                    coach_r2_theoryheavy:   `<strong>Still a lecture course.</strong> Under ~35% experiential is barely a shift from the inert 70% theory that produced 41% transfer. Participants still won't get enough practice to change behaviour.`,
                    coach_r2_stilltheory:   `<strong>A modest shift toward experience.</strong> Better than the inert 70% theory, but you may still be light on the practice that actually moves behaviour. Push a little further toward experience without over-stretching.`,
                    coach_r2_spaced:        `<strong>Spacing is the right arc.</strong> Cycling input, practice and reflection across the three intensives and ending on application beats a one-shot. Now make sure the theory-to-experience balance supports it.`,
                    coach_r2_spacedfit:     `<strong>This is the journey done right.</strong> Spaced cycles of input, practice and reflection at a balanced ratio, ending on application and commitment. That is how the spacing effect and the Kolb cycle turn satisfaction into behaviour change.`,
                    coach_r2_frontload:     `<strong>You've front-loaded the theory.</strong> Every concept in intensive one, projects saved for the end — by the time participants apply anything the theory is six weeks cold and momentum is gone. Spacing says practice should follow input closely, not months later.`,
                    coach_r2_noframing:     `<strong>All experience, no framing, loses the learning.</strong> Drop the conceptual input entirely and participants practise without a model to make sense of it — engagement and coherence both suffer. Experience needs reflection and conceptualisation around it.`,
                    coach_r2_nomap:         `<strong>Unmapped modules are how 'interesting but useless' creeps in.</strong> Without constructive alignment you can't tell which of the five outcomes each module serves — or that one is being missed entirely. Map every module to an outcome.`,
                    coach_r3_project:       `<strong>You funded the action-learning project.</strong> It is the single biggest transfer driver in the kit — +15 points — and yes, it cost 20 faculty-days and some masterclass time. That is exactly the trade the sponsors are measuring. Well made.`,
                    coach_r3_half:          `<strong>A half-project hedges the bet.</strong> You get a partial transfer lift for half the faculty-days — defensible under the envelope, but you've left some of your biggest lever on the table.`,
                    coach_r3_cut:           `<strong>You cut your biggest transfer driver.</strong> Dropping the action-learning project to free days for masterclasses protects content coverage and sacrifices the one thing sponsors are measuring — an estimated +15 points. Was that the trade you meant to make?`,
                    coach_r3_nocoach:       `<strong>You've squeezed coaching and reflection.</strong> They produce no slides, so they're the easy cut — but they are exactly where participants rehearse the new behaviour and get feedback. Protect that time or transfer stalls.`,
                    coach_r3_seniorheavy:   `<strong>Good project — but you've loaded it with senior professors.</strong> They cost roughly twice the faculty-days and pull the journey back toward lecture. Practitioners and coaches deliver more of the transfer work per day; spend the scarce senior time where it uniquely counts.`,
                    coach_r4_leadlag:       `<strong>Leading plus lagging measurement is the right spine.</strong> 360 re-test, manager observation and a transfer index at 3 and 6 months prove behaviour change — and leading indicators during the program tell you it's slipping before the cohort ends, not after.`,
                    coach_r4_endsurvey:     `<strong>The same happy sheet hid the problem last time.</strong> An end-of-program survey measures reaction, not behaviour — it gave you 4.6/5 and 41% transfer side by side. Measure behaviour at work, at 3 and 6 months, or you'll never know if the redesign worked.`,
                    coach_r4_launch:        `<strong>A launch dashboard then moving on is the classic decay pattern.</strong> You promised the Dean transfer would rise to 65%. How will anyone know months later? Measurement has to outlive the launch or you're back to the happy sheet that hid the gap.`,
                    coach_r4_stretch:       `<strong>That defends both constraints at once.</strong> Cohort cohesion, real-work relevance and early wins keep energy high while the stretch does its work — and your measurement proves the transfer is real. Engagement and behaviour change in the same room.`,
                    coach_r4_soften:        `<strong>Softening the hard projects protects the score and kills the transfer.</strong> The stretch is where behaviour changes; quietly removing it to keep enjoyment up rebuilds the very program that scored 4.6 and changed nothing.`,
                    coach_r4_dismiss:       `<strong>Telling the sponsor enjoyment doesn't matter loses the room.</strong> Transfer doesn't happen if leaders disengage and stop attending — the rival school is winning on exactly this. Engagement and transfer are both constraints, not one dial to push.`,
                    coach_r4_restore:       `<strong>Restoring the lectures to placate faculty unwinds the redesign.</strong> You've quietly reverted toward the 70%-theory journey that produced 41% transfer. Defend the cut with the outcome mapping and the evidence — don't surrender it.`,
                    coach_r4_ignore:        `<strong>Pushing it through by authority breeds quiet resistance.</strong> Faculty who feel their content was cut without a hearing will deliver the redesign half-heartedly. Bring them the mapping and redeploy them into coaching and projects.`,

